Port `src/shared/telemetry/{telemetry,for-repo}.ts` (256 + 60 lines).
```rust
pub const FLUSH_AT_RECORDS: usize = 32;
pub const FLUSH_AT_MS: u64 = 5_000;
pub struct Recorder { /* install_id, session_id, seq, buffer */ }
pub fn create(opts: &TelemetryOptions) -> Recorder;
pub fn null() -> Recorder;         // every method a no-op; never touches the filesystem
impl Recorder { pub fn record_call(&mut self, ctx: &RecordContext, spec: &CallSpec);
                pub fn record_index_run(&mut self, ctx: &RecordContext, spec: &IndexRunSpec);
                pub fn flush(&mut self); }
pub fn telemetry_for_repo(input: &RepoTelemetryInput) -> Recorder;
```
Flush triggers: 32 buffered records OR the oldest buffered record is at least 5000 ms old; `seq` increments per session and is written into every record; a flush groups by the record's own UTC date and calls the sink once per date. `telemetry_for_repo` applies the gate `consent && registered && repo_flag` (all three are inputs, none is an env read) and returns `null()` when closed.
Tests: a 31-record buffer not flushed, the 32nd flushed once, an age-triggered flush, `seq` monotonicity across a flush, both sides of the repository gate, and `null()` writing nothing to a temp dir.

Port src/shared/telemetry/report.ts (643 lines).
```rust
pub struct TelemetryReadResult { pub files: usize, pub records: usize, pub unparsed: usize, pub data: ReportData }
pub fn read_records(dir: &Path, since: Option<Since>) -> TelemetryReadResult;
pub fn parse_since(raw: &str, now: DateTime<Utc>) -> Result<Since>;
pub fn aggregate(records: &[TelemetryRecord]) -> ReportData;
```
parse_since accepts exactly the two forms the TypeScript side accepts (src/shared/telemetry/report.ts:121-136): a calendar date YYYY-MM-DD, and a relative Nd. Anything else is a usage refusal with exit 2 — do not add hours or weeks, which the CLI help does not offer either (`bin/sangkar.ts:73` prints --since <YYYY-MM-DD|Nd>).
Reader rules: a line counts as unparsed when it is not JSON, has v != 1, or misses a per-kind required field; the required-field table is copied verbatim. since filters by the record's own ts. bun_version is parsed but never used — no field of the report depends on it.
ReportData must carry the same sections the page renders: totals (records, calls, index runs, sessions, unparsed lines, install ids, days), per-tool stats (tool, calls, statuses, error_codes, p50, p95, mean, max, truncated, mean budget ratio, eq, gte), truncation counts, total-relation counts, coverage counts, argument-name frequencies, the per-day unresolved trend, index-run rows, and session breakdowns (mode, root kind, size bucket).
Percentile method, rounding and bucket order must match the TypeScript implementation: copy the code, do not re-derive a better percentile.
Tests: a directory with a valid file, a file with one malformed line, a since filter, a rejected since value, and an aggregate over two tools whose p50/p95 are asserted exactly.

Port `src/shared/telemetry/{html,html-multi}.ts` (563 + 111 lines).
```rust
pub fn render_body(data: &ReportData) -> String;
pub fn render_html(data: &ReportData, generated_at: &str, since: Option<&str>) -> String;
pub fn render_multi_report(sections: &[ReportSection]) -> String;
```
Panel order is the contract: `<header class="panel">` (h1 `sangkar telemetry`, the window/generated line, and `dl.totals`), then `<section id=…>` for `tools`, `truncation`, `relations`, `coverage`, `argument-names`, `trend`, `index-runs`, `sessions`, and an `empty` panel appended only when `totals.records == 0`. The trend bars are scaled by an inline `<script>` to the window maximum, as today.
Every interpolated value must be HTML-escaped; a record field containing `<` must not break the page (a test asserts it). No framework, no `fetch`, no external asset: one `<style>` with a `prefers-color-scheme: dark` block, copied as-is.
The multi variant emits one section per registered repository, labelled `<basename> — <root>`, reusing `render_body` per section.
Tests: render a fixture `ReportData` and assert the panel ids in order, the absence of an `empty` panel when records exist, its presence when none do, escaping of a hostile string, and a byte-size sanity bound (the page stays under 200 KB for the fixture).
